bullet("  - *Row verdict,*", """  - *Row verdict,* d = ours - reference on identical frames inside the claim region, interval = paired scene bootstrap (1000 draws, by scene); d1 at threshold 0.5 for both (the quoted table), d2 with each system at its own best of {0.3, 0.5, 0.7} (in-sample: the thresholds are chosen once on all frames, so the interval of d2 is conditional on that choice, labelled in the table). **BEYOND** when min(d1, d2) >= +m_row and the lower end of the corresponding interval is above 0; **BEHIND** when max(d1, d2) <= -m_row and the upper end of the corresponding interval is below 0; **NON-INFERIOR** (what is not BEYOND) when the lower end of the interval of min(d1, d2) is above -m_row (at or above the reference within the margin: a ONE-SIDED claim, which is what "at SOA" means); everything else **NOT SEPARATED**. An equivalence ("LEVEL": the whole interval inside +-m_row) was dropped after the measurement: the half-widths of the base's d1 interval on the 43 scenes are crossing 0.046, walkway 0.034, car park 0.087, lane divider 0.052, road divider 0.060, drivable 0.017, against margins of 0.030 (walkway 0.054), so four rows could not be inside +-m at any true d. **Consequence, stated here so that nobody reads more into the verdict:** NON-INFERIOR needs d above -m_row plus the half-width, i.e. above about +0.057 on car park, +0.030 on road divider, +0.022 on lane divider, +0.016 on crossing (walkway -0.020, drivable -0.013): on four rows the 43-scene table can show only BEHIND, NOT SEPARATED or a result at or above the reference; AT SOA is shown only by being at or above the reference by more than the noise.""")

bullet("  - *SOA verdict*", """  - *SOA verdict* (by hand: composed from the table rows; provisional, one seed): **BEYOND SOA** = every row NON-INFERIOR or BEYOND, the object row and at least three map rows BEYOND; **AT SOA** = no row BEHIND and no row NOT SEPARATED (every one of the six map rows and the object row NON-INFERIOR or BEYOND); **UNDECIDED on <rows>** = no row BEHIND but some NOT SEPARATED (there are no more frames in these 43 scenes, and GaussianCaR's logits do not exist for other scenes: the action is a second seed, on Mirko's word); otherwise **BEHIND on <rows>**, each with the share of the gap to the reference that is closed ((ours - base) / (reference - base) on the same frames; the base only reads as a gap where reference minus base is above m_row). If the CRN gate fails: "maps only".""")

bullet("- **Prediction (the lead's", """- **Prediction (the lead's, written before the run; restated 2026-11-15 on the measured base gaps).** Against the base: `detf` mAP +0.03 (0.00 .. +0.07). Measured gaps to GaussianCaR (d1 of the base, section above): crossing 0.352, walkway 0.241, car park 0.261, lane divider 0.286 (d2 0.362), road divider 0.109 (d2 0.171), drivable 0.041. **Share of the gap closed, derived and not guessed:** the old chain's learning curve with frozen features needed 4 to 10 more doublings of the budget to close the gap (t6 report, archive tag), and this run gives 3.7 times the passes of the base (24.2 against 6.6), i.e. 1.9 doublings: 19 - 48 % on that curve; a maps-only run with frozen features closed about a tenth; the trainable encoder is expected to steepen the curve. One share for every row, as there is no row-specific evidence: **30 % (10 .. 50 %).** That predicts d1 after the run: crossing -0.246 (-0.317 .. -0.176), walkway -0.169 (-0.217 .. -0.121), car park -0.183 (-0.235 .. -0.131), lane divider -0.200 (-0.257 .. -0.143), road divider -0.076 (-0.098 .. -0.055), drivable -0.029 (-0.037 .. -0.021). **Expected verdict: BEHIND on crossing, walkway, car park and lane divider; road divider and drivable BEHIND or NOT SEPARATED** (their predicted d1 lies at the margin 0.030, and their interval half-widths are 0.060 and 0.017). The object row against CRN: the forecast is written after the CRN gates and the CRN score of the base, before the launch. The lead's chance for AT SOA is about 1 %, for BEYOND SOA below 1 %. The statement is expected to fail; the run measures by how much, and what the slope says (next bullet).""")

bullet("- **Learning curve from the one run.**", """- **Learning curve from the one run.** The checkpoints at one sixth, one third, two thirds and all of the steps (6000, 12000, 24000, 36000; 4000, 8000, 16000, 24000 under the fall-back) are kept (`keep_steps`, section 2) and read on the 246 CPU frames (diagnostic, by hand). They are points of ONE cosine schedule (the learning rate is 95 / 78 / 26 / 0.1 % of its peak at 6000 / 12000 / 24000 / 36000: `trainkit/schedule.py`), not runs of those lengths: they show whether the curve still rises, they do not replace a budget ablation, and a rise from 12000 to 36000 mixes budget with annealing. **Slope rule (by hand: labelled, diagnostic; the 246-frame read-out has no interval, so only a change of at least 0.02 `detf` mAP counts):** read-out(36000) - read-out(24000) >= +0.02 -> "the budget still pays": the next card is a longer or a larger run; read-out(36000) - read-out(12000) < +0.02 -> "the budget lever is exhausted on this encoder": the next card is the largest remaining row gap (crossing, lane divider) by its own lever; anything in between -> no statement.""")

